{{nihongo|'''Tokuma Ishimoto'''|石本 徳馬|''Ishimoto Tokuma''|14 March 1866 – 19 August 1951}} was a Sanese politician who served as the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|16th Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from November 1937 to August 1939, and later as [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Acting Prime Minister]] from July to September 1940 during the crisis following the [[July 7 Incident]]. An independent politician who led the last non-military government before the [[Taisei Yokusankai]] era, Ishimoto's premiership was defined by the overwhelming dominance of the military over civilian institutions. He was the uncle of [[Kagetora Ishimoto]], who served as the 30th Prime Minister from 1958 to 1960.

Ishimoto assumed office following the collapse of the Kenseikai government of [[Reijiro Wakatsuki]], whose party had been routed in the 1937 general election. As an independent, Ishimoto headed a minority administration that was largely powerless, with key cabinet posts held by military appointees. His government was forced to approve massive military budgets and had little influence over foreign policy, which was increasingly dictated by the Imperial General Staff. On November 14, 1938, Ishimoto was seriously wounded in an assassination attempt by militarist extremists and was incapacitated for nearly four months, during which Foreign Minister [[Shidehara Kijuro]] served as Deputy Prime Minister. Ishimoto returned to office in March 1939 but resigned in August of that year, unable to restrain the military's growing power.

Following the assassination of Prime Minister [[Matsu Noba]] in the July 7 Incident of 1940, Ishimoto was recalled to serve as Acting Prime Minister for two months, providing interim leadership until the appointment of [[Fumio Goto]] as the first prime minister under the Taisei Yokusankai one-party system.

== Early life and education ==
Tokuma Ishimoto was born on 14 March 1866 in [[Osaki, Seto|Osaki]], a city in [[Seto Prefecture]], to a politically prominent family with deep roots in the southern Yagikoshu region. The Ishimoto family had produced several generations of local officials and had connections to the moderate reformist wing of the former samurai establishment. His nephew, [[Kagetora Ishimoto]], would later serve as the 30th Prime Minister of Okaiken from 1958 to 1960.

Ishimoto pursued higher education at [[Okami University]] in the capital, where he studied law and political economy. During his university years, he developed connections with future leaders of the Kenseikai and moderate reformist circles. After graduating in 1889, he entered the civil service as a bureaucrat in the Ministry of Home Affairs, where he developed a reputation for administrative competence and a cautious, pragmatic approach to governance.

== Political career ==
Ishimoto entered electoral politics in the 1910s, winning a seat in the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]] as an independent. He aligned himself with the moderate, pro-constitution faction that sought to preserve civilian control over the military and maintain the principles of [[Go-Shoko Democracy|Go-Shōkō Democracy]], the period of relatively democratic governance that had begun in the 1910s.

Throughout the 1920s and 1930s, Ishimoto served in several junior ministerial positions and gained a reputation as a reliable, if uncharismatic, administrator. He was known for his quiet opposition to the growing influence of the military in politics, though he lacked the political base to challenge it directly. As the Kenseikai party—the last major party to hold the premiership—declined in influence, Ishimoto positioned himself as a non-partisan figure acceptable to both the moderate civilian establishment and the military leadership.

== Premiership (1937–1939) ==
{{Main|Ishimoto Cabinet (1937)}}

[[File:Ishimoto Cabinet 1937.png|thumb|right|200px|The Ishimoto Cabinet in November 1937, shortly after its formation.]]

Ishimoto was appointed Prime Minister on 11 November 1937, following the resignation of [[Reijiro Wakatsuki]] after the Kenseikai's devastating defeat in the 1937 general election. As an independent without a party base, Ishimoto was chosen as a compromise candidate acceptable to both the remnants of the civilian political establishment and the military, which had come to dominate the selection process.

=== Domestic policy ===
Ishimoto's government was from its inception a largely powerless entity. Key cabinet posts—including the Minister of War, Minister of the Navy, and Minister of Munitions—were held by military appointees who answered to the Imperial General Staff rather than to the Prime Minister. The civilian cabinet members, including Ishimoto himself, found themselves repeatedly overruled on matters of military expenditure and national defense policy.

The Ishimoto government was forced to approve massive military budgets that far exceeded what the civilian leadership considered fiscally responsible. Attempts by Finance Ministry officials to restrain spending were routinely blocked by the military, which threatened to bring down the government if its demands were not met. Ishimoto's inability to control military spending eroded what little authority his administration possessed.

On 14 November 1938, Ishimoto was the target of an assassination plot by militarist extremists associated with the [[Cherry Blossom Society]], who viewed his government as insufficiently committed to the expansion of military power. Ishimoto was seriously wounded in the attack and was incapacitated for nearly four months. During his convalescence, Foreign Minister [[Shidehara Kijuro]] served as Deputy Prime Minister, managing the day-to-day affairs of the government while the military effectively operated without civilian oversight.

Ishimoto returned to the office on 10 March 1939, but his authority had been further diminished by his absence. The military had used the period of his incapacitation to consolidate its control over the bureaucracy and the security apparatus, leaving the Prime Minister with even less leverage than before.

=== Foreign policy ===
Foreign policy under the Ishimoto government was effectively dictated by the military, with the civilian Foreign Ministry reduced to a supporting role. The primary strategic concerns of the period were the escalating tensions with [[Shendan]] states over maritime influence and the growing [[Illypnian]] encirclement of Okaiken's overseas interests.

The military-dominated foreign policy pursued a confrontational stance toward the [[Illypnian]] powers, particularly [[Volisania]] and [[Breisland]], which had been expanding their naval presence in the Shendan Ocean. Ishimoto's government was compelled to endorse diplomatic positions it privately considered reckless, including the reinforcement of forward positions that further strained relations with the Illypnian coalition.

The [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] continued to shape the strategic calculus, with military planners viewing the Zong alliance as essential to countering Illypnian naval superiority. Ishimoto's attempts to pursue a more conciliatory diplomatic approach were consistently undermined by the Imperial General Staff, which conducted its own parallel diplomacy with Zong military officials.

== Acting Prime Minister (1940) ==
{{Main|July 7 Incident}}

[[File:July 7 Incident Okami 1940.png|thumb|right|200px|The aftermath of the July 7 Incident in Okami, which claimed the life of Prime Minister Matsu Noba.]]

Following the assassination of Prime Minister [[Matsu Noba]] in the [[July 7 Incident]] on 7 July 1940, Okaiken was plunged into a political crisis. Noba, a Navy Admiral who had been brought in as a compromise candidate, had overseen the dissolution of all political parties into the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]]. His assassination by extremists from the [[Cherry Blossom Society]] left the nation without a head of government during a period of extreme instability.

The Emperor, acting on the advice of the Privy Council, appointed Ishimoto as Acting Prime Minister on 7 July 1940. Ishimoto's selection was based on his experience as a former Prime Minister and his perceived neutrality in the factional struggles between the military's ''Toseiha'' (Control Faction) and ''Kodoha'' (Imperial Way Faction). His primary task was to maintain governmental continuity and prevent a complete breakdown of civil order.

During his two months as Acting Prime Minister, Ishimoto worked to stabilize the political situation and facilitate the transition to a new permanent government. He oversaw the initial implementation of the Taisei Yokusankai one-party system that Noba had established before his death, ensuring that the basic functions of state continued to operate. On 14 September 1940, Ishimoto handed over power to [[Fumio Goto]], a career bureaucrat who became the first Prime Minister to serve under the Taisei Yokusankai system.

== Later life and death ==
After his tenure as Acting Prime Minister, Ishimoto largely withdrew from active politics. He remained a member of the House of Peers but played no significant role in the Taisei Yokusankai-dominated politics of the 1940s. During the [[Great Adonian War]], he maintained a low profile, avoiding the public scrutiny that had marked his earlier career.

Ishimoto's nephew, [[Kagetora Ishimoto]], would later enter politics and serve as Prime Minister from 1958 to 1960, leading the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] during the early post-war reconstruction period. The Ishimoto brothers are one of only two pairs of brothers to have both served as Prime Minister of Okaiken, alongside the [[Honda Tadashige|Honda]] brothers.

Tokuma Ishimoto died on 19 August 1951 in [[Okami]] at the age of 85. He was survived by his wife and children. His funeral was attended by a small number of political figures, reflecting his diminished stature in the post-war political landscape.

== Legacy ==
Historians generally regard Ishimoto's premiership as emblematic of the collapse of civilian government during the militarist era. His inability to control the military, his near-fatal assassination, and his reduced role as Acting Prime Minister during the transition to one-party rule collectively illustrate the systematic dismantling of democratic institutions that characterized the late 1930s and early 1940s in Okaiken.

Ishimoto is often compared unfavorably to his predecessor [[Takaaki Kato]], who had at least attempted to build a coalition to resist military encroachment, and to his successor [[Shishido Hayashi]], whose assassination in the October 2 Incident demonstrated the lethal consequences of opposition. Ishimoto's more passive approach is seen by some scholars as a pragmatic survival strategy and by others as a capitulation that accelerated the end of party government.

The Ishimoto family's political legacy was partially redeemed by his nephew [[Kagetora Ishimoto]], whose premiership in the late 1950s is remembered more positively for its role in Okaiken's post-war economic redevelopment. The contrast between the two brothers' tenures—Tokuma's powerless government under military dominance and Kagetora's technocratic administration during the economic boom—has made the Ishimoto brothers a frequent subject of comparative political analysis.

